#: source/language/derivations.md:1
msgid "Derivations"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:3
msgid ""
"The most important built-in function is `derivation`, which is used to "
"describe a single store-layer [store derivation](../glossary.md#gloss-store-"
"derivation). Consult the [store chapter](../store/derivation/index.md) for "
"what a store derivation is; this section just concerns how to create one "
"from the Nix language."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:7
msgid ""
"This builtin function takes as input an attribute set, the attributes of "
"which specify the inputs to the process. It outputs an attribute set, and "
"produces a [store derivation](../glossary.md#gloss-store-derivation) as a "
"side effect of evaluation."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:12
msgid "Input attributes"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:14
msgid "Required"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:16
msgid "\\[`name`\\]{#attr-name} ([String](../language/types.md#type-string))"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:18
msgid ""
"A symbolic name for the derivation. See [derivation outputs](../store/"
"derivation/outputs/index.md#outputs) for what this is affects."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:23 source/language/derivations.md:39
#: source/language/derivations.md:51 source/language/derivations.md:69
#: source/language/derivations.md:83 source/language/derivations.md:97
#: source/language/derivations.md:118 source/language/derivations.md:142
#: source/language/derivations.md:170 source/language/derivations.md:191
msgid "**Example**"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:25
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"derivation {\n"
"  name = \"hello\";\n"
"  # ...\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:32
msgid ""
"The derivation's path will be `/nix/store/<hash>-hello.drv`. The [output]"
"(#attr-outputs) paths will be of the form `/nix/store/<hash>-hello[-"
"<output>]`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:35
msgid ""
"\\[`system`\\]{#attr-system} ([String](../language/types.md#type-string))"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:37
msgid "See [system](../store/derivation/index.md#system)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:41
msgid "Declare a derivation to be built on a specific system type:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:43
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"derivation {\n"
"  # ...\n"
"  system = \"x86_64-linux\";\n"
"  # ...\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:53
msgid ""
"Declare a derivation to be built on the system type that evaluates the "
"expression:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:63
msgid ""
"[`builtins.currentSystem`](../language/builtins.md#builtins-currentSystem) "
"has the value of the \\[`system` configuration option\\], and defaults to "
"the system type of the current Nix installation."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:65
msgid ""
"\\[`builder`\\]{#attr-builder} ([Path](../language/types.md#type-path) | "
"[String](../language/types.md#type-string))"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:67
msgid "See [builder](../store/derivation/index.md#builder)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:71
msgid "Use the file located at `/bin/bash` as the builder executable:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:73
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"derivation {\n"
"  # ...\n"
"  builder = \"/bin/bash\";\n"
"  # ...\n"
"};\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:85
msgid "Copy a local file to the Nix store for use as the builder executable:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:99
msgid "Use a file from another derivation as the builder executable:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:101
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let pkgs = import <nixpkgs> {}; in\n"
"derivation {\n"
"  # ...\n"
"  builder = \"${pkgs.python}/bin/python\";\n"
"  # ...\n"
"};\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:110
msgid "Optional"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:112
msgid ""
"\\[`args`\\]{#attr-args} ([List](../language/types.md#type-list) of [String]"
"(../language/types.md#type-string))"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:114
msgid "Default: `[ ]`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:116
msgid "See [args](../store/derivation/index.md#args)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:120
msgid "Pass arguments to Bash to interpret a shell command:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:122
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"derivation {\n"
"  # ...\n"
"  builder = \"/bin/bash\";\n"
"  args = [ \"-c\" \"echo hello world > $out\" ];\n"
"  # ...\n"
"};\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:131
msgid ""
"\\[`outputs`\\]{#attr-outputs} ([List](../language/types.md#type-list) of "
"[String](../language/types.md#type-string))"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:133
msgid "Default: `[ \"out\" ]`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:135
msgid ""
"Symbolic outputs of the derivation. Each output name is passed to the "
"[`builder`](#attr-builder) executable as an environment variable with its "
"value set to the corresponding [store path](../store/store-path.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:138
msgid ""
"By default, a derivation produces a single output called `out`. However, "
"derivations can produce multiple outputs. This allows the associated [store "
"objects](../store/store-object.md) and their [closures](../glossary.md#gloss-"
"closure) to be copied or garbage-collected separately."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:144
msgid ""
"Imagine a library package that provides a dynamic library, header files, and "
"documentation. A program that links against such a library doesn’t need the "
"header files and documentation at runtime, and it doesn’t need the "
"documentation at build time. Thus, the library package could specify:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:148
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"derivation {\n"
"  # ...\n"
"  outputs = [ \"lib\" \"dev\" \"doc\" ];\n"
"  # ...\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:156
msgid ""
"This will cause Nix to pass environment variables `lib`, `dev`, and `doc` to "
"the builder containing the intended store paths of each output. The builder "
"would typically do something like"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:166
msgid "for an Autoconf-style package."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:168
msgid ""
"The name of an output is combined with the name of the derivation to create "
"the [name part](../store/store-path.md#name) of the output's store path, "
"unless it is `out`, in which case just the name of the derivation is used."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:173
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"derivation {\n"
"  name = \"example\";\n"
"  outputs = [ \"lib\" \"dev\" \"doc\" \"out\" ];\n"
"  # ...\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:181
msgid ""
"The store derivation path will be `/nix/store/<hash>-example.drv`. The "
"output paths will be"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:183
msgid "`/nix/store/<hash>-example-lib`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:184
msgid "`/nix/store/<hash>-example-dev`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:185
msgid "`/nix/store/<hash>-example-doc`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:186
msgid "`/nix/store/<hash>-example`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:188
msgid ""
"You can refer to each output of a derivation by selecting it as an "
"attribute. The first element of `outputs` determines the _default output_ "
"and ends up at the top-level."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:193
msgid "Select an output by attribute name:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:195
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let\n"
"  myPackage = derivation {\n"
"    name = \"example\";\n"
"    outputs = [ \"lib\" \"dev\" \"doc\" \"out\" ];\n"
"    # ...\n"
"  };\n"
"in myPackage.dev\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:205
msgid ""
"Since `lib` is the first output, `myPackage` is equivalent to `myPackage."
"lib`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:209
msgid ""
"See [Advanced Attributes](./advanced-attributes.md) for more, infrequently "
"used, optional attributes."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:213
msgid ""
"Every other attribute is passed as an environment variable to the builder. "
"Attribute values are translated to environment variables as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:216
msgid "Strings are passed unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:218
msgid "Integral numbers are converted to decimal notation."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:220
msgid ""
"Floating point numbers are converted to simple decimal or scientific "
"notation with a preset precision."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:222
msgid ""
"A _path_ (e.g., `../foo/sources.tar`) causes the referenced file to be "
"copied to the store; its location in the store is put in the environment "
"variable. The idea is that all sources should reside in the Nix store, since "
"all inputs to a derivation should reside in the Nix store."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:228
msgid ""
"A _derivation_ causes that derivation to be built prior to the present "
"derivation. The environment variable is set to the [store path](../store/"
"store-path.md) of the derivation's default [output](#attr-outputs)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:231
msgid ""
"Lists of the previous types are also allowed. They are simply concatenated, "
"separated by spaces."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:234
msgid ""
"`true` is passed as the string `1`, `false` and `null` are passed as an "
"empty string."
msgstr ""
